From ae048ade05ee715f26c1122484abe6eb5bfe6fc6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 22:58:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] analysis: protocol-invariant detector (inferred aggregate ==
 sum of ledger conservation)

The first meaning-aware rule of the deterministic Solidity analyzer. The trace now
gives every state write a linear change over symbolic amounts, resolved through
helper arguments, storage references and locals into the entry function's terms,
plus the enclosing branch conditions decided statically where syntax or an earlier
unconditional reverting check settles them (so a single _update(from, to, v)
helper is followed per caller).

invariants.ts infers "A == sum over a of L[a]" for a scalar unsigned A and an
address-keyed ledger L (or a struct field of one) only when at least two
functions change both sides by the same non-zero amount, in both directions,
and reports a function that changes them by different amounts, unless another
state quantity absorbs the difference, the call keeps another aggregate equal to
the ledger instead, or A is re-set absolutely. Undecided branches, loops and
non-linear updates are skipped, never reported. Constructors are evidence only.

Findings use the existing machinery (class protocol-invariant), cite the write
and the call chain, state the inferred invariant and the maintaining functions,
and are challenged by execution (challengeInvariant: change of A versus change of
sum(L) in a world seeded consistent, after checking the maintainers keep it).

Synthetic unit tests only (invariants.test.ts): 8 positive, 12 negative, 2 execution-challenge.

